WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.220
Hibernation is a concept

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.307
we're very familiar with.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.733
Many sci-fi stories require
hibernation to work.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.170
While imaginations have been running wild

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.750
in pop culture, the science of hibernation

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.923
and a shorter state called
torpor have been lagging behind.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.830
Now this is changing.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:27.380
An array of potential medical
benefits is driving scientists

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.520
from labs around the world to search

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.393
for the underlying mechanisms
of this near-magical state.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.650
To travel for centuries or for millennia,

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.430
that's way out of our reach at the moment

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.650
but hibernation could
bridge you from a condition

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.370
in which you could not live any longer

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:47.060
to one in which you may still live.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.950
As well as helping to buy time for people

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.970
on the waiting list for an organ,

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.890
a synthetic form of hibernation

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.630
could also possibly offer
protection against radiation

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.933
to cancer patients and space travelers.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.230
There are serious experiments
that have been done.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:06.480
There are significant and serious results

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.140
that have been produced,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.573
so I would say it's not
science fiction anymore.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.350
Now is the moment, yes.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.130
Now we at least have
a path in front of us,

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:19.173
a research path that we
know could lead there.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.860
Torpor is the actual state
in which our metabolism -

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.270
the energy we use - drops to a minimum,

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.860
so it is a suspended animation state.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.720
Many episodes of torpor,
one after the other,

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.340
interrupted by a brief,
very brief arousal,

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:44.893
that's what makes hibernation.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.460
We have bats that go into hibernation,

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.520
bears, rodents, primates.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.650
Some animals can enter
brief episodes of torpor.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.890
You'd be surprised how many
hamsters get thrown away

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.730
because their owner think they're dead,

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.530
but actually they're just in torpor.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.860
While it might not be
something that humans do,

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.620
hibernation or torpor is common in species

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:11.230
across the many orders of mammals.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.000
This might mean that the
genes involved in triggering

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.370
and controlling these states were present

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.843
in our common ancestor who
needed a survival strategy.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.280
When we first, as mammals,
appeared on this planet,

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.610
we were hunted by
something about this size,

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:35.910
even a bit smaller.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.270
So this is actually an
herbivore, to be honest,

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:42.080
but just imagine like a giant
ostrich or even a turkey

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.623
that were velociraptors and mini-raptors.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.100
Our ancestors developed a new ability:

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.300
generating heat within our
own cells, instead of relying

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.283
on an outside source, like the sun.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:01.800
That helped them hunt at night

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.623
when dinosaurs were less active.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.080
But generating energy to
maintain the body's temperature

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.090
requires resources which might have been

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:11.453
often hard to come by.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.380
The idea to turn off the heater,

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.290
turn off the unnecessary energy
use that your body requires

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:26.770
even when we're resting,
is an energy-saving

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.523
and a life-saving strategy.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.220
There is nothing you're
doing in this state,

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.990
besides surviving.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:36.350
You're bridging a time in which you

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.810
couldn't be alive -
because there was no food

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:41.080
or water or whatever resources you needed,

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.050
get very, very near death

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:46.583
without touching that impenetrable wall.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:50.410
Unlike sleep, in a state of torpor

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.920
the physiology of an
animal changes completely.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:55.840
The majority of cells that produce

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.280
or use energy simply stop,

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.130
and as a result, the body's
temperature goes down

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:03.583
and its oxygen consumption decreases.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.780
The kidneys shut down as they
don't need to expel nitrogen,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.540
and with no food to process,

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.490
the gastrointestinal tract changes.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:14.940
Muscle and bones enter a resting stage

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.490
but don't lose their strength.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.680
With a breath drawn every minute or so,

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.520
the heart is barely beating,

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.400
pumping just enough blood
to keep vital organs alive.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.260
And the most energy-consuming
organ - the brain -

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.783
also changes its rhythm.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:33.200
We don't need the brain
during torpor pretty much.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.390
The synapses get reabsorbed,

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:38.890
meaning neurons will
withdraw their handshake,

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:41.810
and then talk way less to each other.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.690
So they speak with different rhythms,

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:47.500
and those rhythms during
torpor are the same rhythm

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.690
we use when we are awake or at
least they very much resemble

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:54.240
the intense activity that
we use when we are awake,

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.683
but much, much slower.

00:04:56.602 --> 00:05:00.363
It's a slow wakefulness, if
we allow me to pass this term.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.650
Let's say it's like watching a movie,

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.603
and we see the frame of
this movie going slower

00:05:06.603 --> 00:05:09.620
and slower progressively, and at the end,

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.493
we just stick with one single picture.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:18.240
Torpor is very, very different from sleep,

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.370
from coma, from general anesthesia,

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:23.650
from any behavior we think about

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:25.860
like resting or inactivity.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.290
We're not even sure
that torpor is resting.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:31.933
The body needs to recover
from torpor after torpor.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.930
After the dinosaurs disappeared,

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.320
mammals took over the world

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.020
and many species lost this
once life-saving ability.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.520
But restoring it presents
an exciting possibility.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.410
Hibernation and torpor is something hidden

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:47.730
within our genetic code

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:49.880
and within our brain regulation, probably.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:53.150
And we think that today we have lost maybe

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:56.570
the ability to initiate this behavior

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:59.050
because we didn't need it for a long time.

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:02.647
But we are trying to discover
what is the ignition key.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.760
Here, at one of the oldest universities

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.363
in the world, Matteo Cerri
is a professor of physiology.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:16.010
In 2013, he and his team were the first

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:19.490
to induce what he calls
synthetic torpor in an animal

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:22.103
that doesn't naturally
enter this state - a rat.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.390
It was just by accident.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:28.360
I worked before on
metabolism and the idea was

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.090
to increase metabolism to lose weight.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.730
One day we just tried
to see what could happen

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:36.950
if we were actually
shutting down metabolism.

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:39.240
We block, we inhibit a
small group of neurons

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.960
in a very old region of the
brain called raphe pallidus.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:45.170
The raphe pallidus is
a very interesting area

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:46.310
of the brain stem.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:49.080
It controls a lot of our body function;

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.160
the heart rate, body temperature,
respiration, breathing.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.870
So it's the area we use to
defend ourself from the cold.

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:59.010
It makes you breathe faster
because you need more oxygen

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:00.690
to sustain heat production.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.910
We were sure if we were stopping
that flow of information,

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.450
we could see torpor, and yes, we did.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.740
The first experiment worked
and it was very lucky,

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.190
it never happens the first time.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:14.090
Still one of the happiest
moments in my professional life.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:20.260
With this thermal camera,

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:23.880
we can record the
temperature of the surface.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:28.880
Coolest is blue and black,
the red and white is warmer.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:39.500
Using drugs to switch off

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.020
this part of the brain,
the team bring the animal

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:44.470
in and out of a torpor-like state.

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:47.710
Just like natural torpor, the
animal's temperature drops

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.883
and a period of inactivity follows.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:53.840
Once the animal is brought out of it,

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:55.440
it's as though nothing happened.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:59.790
Scientists have now found even more ways

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.010
of inducing this state artificially,

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:03.760
and many teams are investigating

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:05.973
the body's adaptations during torpor.

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:09.500
In Germany, a team led
by Walter Tinganelli

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:13.003
is using a particle accelerator
to further this research.

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:26.210
We try to understand
the molecular mechanism

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:29.030
behind the hibernation itself.

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:34.030
So we try to simulate in-vitro,
so on cells, cell culture,

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:37.170
the physiological change
at which the cells

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:38.443
are during hibernation.

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:45.720
A complex process like torpor

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.170
can't be simply induced in a petri dish,

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.410
but the team can simulate its conditions

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:51.703
on a cellular level.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:57.730
We perform experiments changing
the oxygen concentration

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:00.970
of the cells, changing
the metabolism of cells.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:04.170
For example, producing starvation on cells

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:06.623
or changing the temperature, of course.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:10.150
Although this research into hibernation

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:12.260
relies on cutting-edge science,

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:14.923
its foundations can be
traced back decades.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.260
The most important
experiment with hibernators,

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:25.700
they happen around the '60s

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.400
when the first human space missions start.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.270
With Apollo 11, of course,
they take the astronauts

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:33.123
on the moon in 1969.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:37.210
Because the physiological changes

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:40.060
that happen in natural hibernators

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.643
are very interesting for space.

00:09:44.503 --> 00:09:47.140
And then what the researchers found out

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:49.440
was that the radioresistance of an animal

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.100
during hibernation increases.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:56.250
But then the idea was okay,
but if humans cannot hibernate,

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.590
why we should continue
this kind of experiment,

00:09:58.590 --> 00:09:59.720
such an experiment.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:03.560
And then the interest,
let's say, on hibernation

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:05.990
was fading a little bit.

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:11.420
Developments in the past decade

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.370
have revived interest in the resistance

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:15.650
to radiation while in torpor.

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:18.180
The team at GSI is particularly interested

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:19.950
in how this could one day be harnessed

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:22.253
in radiotherapy for cancer patients.

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:26.290
You want to kill the tumor
and spare the healthy tissue.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:29.510
If you can bring the patients
into synthetic hibernation,

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:31.740
then first of all, most
probably you will stop

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:32.880
the tumor growth.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.370
But then if you are also able to reduce

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:38.170
the healthy tissue damage
and to treat with, let's say,

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:40.530
a higher than today dose,

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:44.200
this could be in future,
perhaps a very nice tool.

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.610
And we try to understand if this process

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:49.270
increases the radio-resistance just

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:52.293
in the healthy tissue and
not in the tumor tissue.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:55.370
And so a few times a year,

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:58.730
cancer cells in a state of
simulated synthetic torpor,

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.330
like these taken from a mouse,

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:03.220
are put into one of GSI's caves.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:04.610
These are rooms where outlets

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:06.600
from the particle accelerator lead,

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:10.310
and so the cells are
irradiated with heavy ions.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:14.380
Here is where we put our flask with cells.

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:17.160
You do not shoot a radioactive particle.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.510
You just shoot the ion, so it's an atom

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:24.040
without some electrons, and
then this does a certain damage,

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:26.463
especially at the end of its track.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:30.360
Along the lines where the ions

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.540
have pierced the cell, the
molecules, highlighted in green,

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:36.110
are trying to mend the damaged DNA.

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:39.060
Torpor doesn't stop cell
damage but one theory is

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:42.280
that the repair might be
carried out more smoothly.

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:45.670
That cell is not really
working because it's in torpor.

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:48.780
It's like if you had to
fix a car that broke,

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:50.640
it would be much harder to fix a car

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.290
while the engine was running,
while the car was running.

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:57.140
And that's why we think
cells in torpor are better

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:58.993
at dealing with radiation damage.

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.210
After irradiation, the team analyzes

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:08.750
how many cells survive and
which genes were involved.

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:11.930
Then you can play with
these cells, changing

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:14.750
for example, silencing a specific gene.

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.550
And then to understand
which of these genes

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:21.140
are very important for the
synthetic hibernation process,

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:22.900
for increasing the radioresistance.

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:25.890
So playing with all these different tools,

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:29.100
we will be able to understand
as much as possible

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.983
about this very complex process.

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:37.210
GSI's ability to bombard cells

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:41.260
with heavy ions also has
applications for space radiation,

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:43.790
one of the main problems agencies like ESA

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.850
and NASA need to overcome
for human exploration

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:48.023
of the solar system.

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.200
In space, the most dangerous radiation

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:55.130
are essentially particles,
so 70% are protons,

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:57.090
very energetic protons.

00:12:57.090 --> 00:13:01.360
But then you have a lot
of ions up to the iron,

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:06.353
so that really hits the astronaut
like a small projectile.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:09.480
During a long voyage to Mars,

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.260
synthetic torpor could
offer added protection,

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:14.870
not only from radiation,
but also from loss

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:17.070
of bone and muscle strength.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:19.600
And there would be other
practical benefits.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.410
No need for extra food,
or to mitigate the stress

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:23.833
of prolonged isolation.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:28.490
And as the team continues to
observe the impact of torpor

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:31.190
on the body and mind,
there could also be clues

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.710
as to how we tackle and
perhaps even reverse

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.073
other forms of degeneration here on Earth.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:42.950
In the brain during torpor,
some markers start to appear.

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:44.113
What am I'm saying with markers?

00:13:44.113 --> 00:13:49.113
Markers are an indication that
something usually is wrong.

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:51.530
We use markers to identify diseases.

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:54.210
One of those markers is a hallmark

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:55.960
of Alzheimer's disease.

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:59.020
The brain of a squirrel in torpor

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:02.870
will drastically resemble the
brain of an Alzheimer patient.

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:06.183
We could say hibernation
is a dive in dementia.

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:09.630
What is interesting is
that when animals arouse

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:13.720
from torpor and hibernation,
their brain very quickly

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:15.050
returns to normal.

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:18.710
Synapses again reform in
stronger numbers eventually.

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:20.270
Some evidence suggests that the brain

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:22.773
works even better after torpor.

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:27.830
The arousal from torpor
is a neurotrophic storm.

00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:31.200
To be honest, it's the most
intense neural plasticity

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:32.060
I've ever seen.

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:33.713
The brain is really rewiring.

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:39.290
Could we exploit and understand
what is the mechanism

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:42.360
behind this extremely intense plasticity

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:45.310
and apply it to a
neurodegenerative disorder?

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:48.800
Or could we manipulate
memories or neural trace

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.560
or behavior that we have
learned, like addiction

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:54.370
and kind of prevent
themselves to be rewired?

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:57.280
This is all unknown,
but it's quite possible.

00:14:57.280 --> 00:14:59.710
With trials so far only done in rodents,

00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:01.670
there are still questions to be answered

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:03.400
before moving forward.

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:05.180
During natural hibernation,

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:07.517
animals have to come out
of torpor occasionally

00:15:07.517 --> 00:15:09.260
and we don't know why.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:10.900
We also still don't know how long

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:13.140
a synthetic torpor could last safely,

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:14.770
and if humans would need to be brought

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:17.580
out of this state periodically as well.

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:19.560
All of this means that
getting to the stage

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:22.410
of human clinical trials won't be easy.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:26.710
My feeling is that if we're
ever gonna reach the phase

00:15:26.710 --> 00:15:31.300
of testing it in humans,
it will be on some patient

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:34.330
that is really in a
very critical condition,

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:37.030
something that has no treatment at all

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:41.550
and death is looming and
there's no way out of that.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.440
Someone on the waiting
list for a transplant

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:45.890
could be the case, a
very aggressive cancer

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:48.060
could be another case.

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:49.790
But even if synthetic torpor

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:52.240
and hibernation can one day save lives,

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:55.160
and perhaps even smooth
out our road to the stars,

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:57.160
there are still many ethical questions

00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:58.310
that will need answers.

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:03.450
What's the status of a
human in synthetic torpor

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:04.283
for a long time?

00:16:04.283 --> 00:16:06.260
Let's say you stay for a year or two.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:09.118
What if you get divorced by
your partner during torpor?

00:16:09.118 --> 00:16:10.380
You have to be woken up?

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:12.685
Who has the right to wake you up?

00:16:12.685 --> 00:16:15.140
What if the stock market collapses?

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:17.360
What if you were charged with crimes?

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:18.920
What if you were convicted

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:21.210
and then you wanna spend your time

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.380
in prison in synthetic torpor?

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:25.623
So there are plenty of
ethical implications.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:31.090
This research is still
the domain of academia,

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:34.710
but private investment
might not be too far away.

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:38.080
Some private company,
space-related companies,

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:39.880
I'm sure they are interested in that

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.400
because even in conferences sometimes

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:43.560
they come to my colleagues,

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:47.260
they would like to know more
which stage we are and so on.

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:50.410
Something like, okay, it's interesting

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:54.660
but let's leave them to do,
let's say, the dirty job now

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:56.460
and then if we recognize that in the end

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:59.510
something good can come out from that,

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:01.103
perhaps we will jump on it.

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:03.830
The way research is structured today,

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:06.130
you have to compete with your
colleagues to get funding.

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:09.500
This is slowing science down
and is slowing innovation down.

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:12.490
And I'm afraid of this switch
in the focus of research

00:17:12.490 --> 00:17:15.490
from public university, or
from university in general,

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:16.832
to the private sector.

00:17:16.832 --> 00:17:19.100
But maybe that's the way it's gonna go.

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:23.240
I'm just worried that some
critical technology could be

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:28.110
owned and therefore used as
a mean to power eventually.

